+# Publishing the tip jar
+
+The site ships as a static export pinned on IPFS. There is no server: the RPC URL, the chain id and
+the contract address are all baked into the JavaScript at build time, and the resulting CID is
+immutable. Getting one of them wrong means building again and publishing a new CID.
+
+Work through this top to bottom. Nothing here uploads anything until step 5.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Prerequisites
+
+- **Node 20.18.3 – 24.x** (`nvm use` — see `.nvmrc`). Node 25 breaks the Next.js prerender.
+- A deployer keystore with ETH on Base:
+  ```bash
+  yarn generate            # new account, or
+  yarn account:import      # import an existing private key
+  yarn account             # show the address and its balances
+  ```
+- A **Base RPC URL you control**. The shared Scaffold-ETH Alchemy key is rate limited and cannot be
+  rotated once it is inside a published CID.
+- Your own **WalletConnect project id** from <https://cloud.reown.com>.
+
+Put the frontend values in `packages/nextjs/.env.local` (or export them in the build shell):
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL=https://base-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<your-key>
+NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=<your-project-id>
+```
+
+---
+
+## 1. Deploy `TipJar` to Base
+
+```bash
+yarn deploy --network base --file DeployTipJar.s.sol
+```
+
+The script defaults to the canonical Base USDC address
+(`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`) and reverts if there is no token contract there, so
+it cannot silently deploy a jar wired to nothing. The deployer becomes the owner — the address that
+can call `withdraw`.
+
+This also rewrites `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts` with an `8453` entry. **Step 3
+will not type-check until this has run.**
+
+Note the address and confirm it on chain:
+
+```bash
+export JAR=$(jq -r '.transactions[] | select(.contractName=="TipJar") | .contractAddress' \
+  packages/foundry/broadcast/DeployTipJar.s.sol/8453/run-latest.json)
+echo "TipJar: $JAR"
+
+cast call $JAR "usdc()(address)"  -r https://mainnet.base.org   # -> 0x8335...2913
+cast call $JAR "owner()(address)" -r https://mainnet.base.org   # -> your deployer
+```
+
+Optionally verify the source on Basescan (needs `ETHERSCAN_API_KEY` in `packages/foundry/.env`):
+
+```bash
+make -C packages/foundry verify RPC_URL=base
+```
+
+---
+
+## 2. Point the frontend at Base
+
+Edit `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`:
+
+```diff
+-const targetNetwork = chains.foundry;
++const targetNetwork = chains.base;
+```
+
+This is a source edit rather than an environment variable on purpose: Scaffold-ETH derives the
+contract types from this value, so the compiler checks the page against the ABI of the chain you are
+actually shipping to.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Build the static bundle
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+Which is `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true yarn build`, and turns on `output: "export"`,
+`trailingSlash: true` and unoptimised images. Output lands in `packages/nextjs/out/`.
+
+`next.config.ts` refuses to produce this build if the target network is still a local chain, or if
+no RPC is configured for it, and warns if the WalletConnect project id is still the shared default.
+A failure looks like:
+
+```
+❌ This IPFS build is not production ready:
+
+  1. scaffold.config.ts targets Foundry (chain 31337), which is a local chain.
+     Nobody but you can reach it. Set `const targetNetwork = chains.base`.
+```
+
+### Optional: absolute URLs in the social preview tags
+
+Open Graph tags need an absolute URL, and a CID is not known until after the upload. If the site
+will live behind a stable name (DNSLink, ENS, a fixed gateway path), set it and rebuild:
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://tips.example.eth.limo yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+Otherwise leave it unset; everything except the preview image still works.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Check the bundle before you upload
+
+An upload is permanent and public. Two minutes here is cheaper than a stale CID.
+
+**Serve it exactly like a gateway would** and click through it:
+
+```bash
+npx serve packages/nextjs/out -l 4321
+# open http://localhost:4321 -- connect a wallet on Base, confirm the feed loads and
+# that "Tips are paid in USDC on Base" appears under the title
+```
+
+**Confirm what actually got baked in.** These read the built JavaScript, not the source:
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+# The RPC the published site will use. Should be exactly the one you configured, and only that.
+grep -rhoE 'https://[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+/v2/[A-Za-z0-9_-]+' out/_next/static/chunks | sort -u
+
+# Your own WalletConnect project id, not the shared Scaffold-ETH default.
+grep -rl "3a8170812b534d0ff9d794f19a901d64" out/_next/static/chunks && \
+  echo "!! still using the shared WalletConnect project id"
+
+# Base USDC, and the jar address from step 1, are both in the bundle.
+export JAR=<address from step 1>
+grep -rl "833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913" out/_next/static/chunks | head -1
+grep -rli "${JAR#0x}" out/_next/static/chunks | head -1
+```
+
+Do not bother grepping for `31337`: `viem/chains` ships every chain definition, so the local chain
+id is in the bundle no matter what you target. The subtitle in the browser is the check that
+actually tells you which chain the app will use — it is rendered from `scaffold.config.ts`:
+
+```
+Tips are paid in USDC on Base.        # correct
+Tips are paid in USDC on Foundry.     # you skipped step 2
+```
+
+**Compute the CID locally**, without uploading, so you know what you are about to publish:
+
+```bash
+packages/nextjs/node_modules/.bin/ipfs-car pack packages/nextjs/out --output /tmp/tipjar.car
+# prints the root CID, e.g. bafybeih...
+```
+
+The CAR file is also directly uploadable to any CAR-based pinning service. (An `ipfs add` on a
+different node can produce a different root CID if its chunker or CID version settings differ —
+compare like with like.)
+
+---
+
+## 5. Upload
+
+Pick one. All of them publish the contents of `packages/nextjs/out/` and print a CID.
+
+**A — Scaffold-ETH's built-in helper** (builds and uploads to the BuidlGuidl IPFS cluster):
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs
+```
+
+**B — Your own Kubo node** (nothing leaves your infrastructure until you pin it elsewhere):
+
+```bash
+ipfs add -r --cid-version 1 --quieter packages/nextjs/out   # last line is the root CID
+ipfs pin add <CID>
+```
+
+**C — Storacha / web3.storage**, from the CAR built in step 4:
+
+```bash
+npx @web3-storage/w3cli up --car /tmp/tipjar.car
+```
+
+**D — Pinata:**
+
+```bash
+npx pinata-cli -u packages/nextjs/out
+```
+
+Whichever you use, **pin the CID in at least two places.** An unpinned CID is garbage collected and
+the site disappears.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Post-deploy verification
+
+```bash
+export CID=<the CID from step 5>
+export GW="https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link"
+```
+
+Use the **subdomain** form (`https://<cid>.ipfs.<gateway>`), not the path form
+(`https://<gateway>/ipfs/<cid>/`). The export uses absolute asset paths (`/_next/...`), which only
+resolve when the CID is the origin root. Public gateways redirect path URLs to the subdomain form
+for CIDv1 automatically, which is why step 4 builds a v1 CID.
+
+**1. The page is served and is the right page:**
+
+```bash
+curl -sSfL "$GW/" | grep -o '<title>[^<]*</title>'
+# <title>USDC Tip Jar</title>
+```
+
+**2. Its assets resolve** — this is the check that catches a broken gateway or a partial pin:
+
+```bash
+CHUNK=$(curl -sSfL "$GW/" | grep -o '/_next/static/chunks/[^"]*\.js' | head -1)
+curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{size_download}B $CHUNK\n" "$GW$CHUNK"
+# expect 200 and a non-trivial size
+```
+
+**3. Every route is present** (the export writes one directory per route):
+
+```bash
+for p in / /debug/ /blockexplorer/; do
+  printf "%-18s %s\n" "$p" "$(curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$GW$p")"
+done
+```
+
+**4. A missing path 404s rather than hanging** (confirms you are reading a complete directory, not
+a partial pin):
+
+```bash
+curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' "$GW/nope/"    # 404
+```
+
+**5. It is pinned widely enough to survive one gateway going down:**
+
+```bash
+for gw in dweb.link ipfs.io cf-ipfs.com 4everland.io; do
+  printf "%-16s %s\n" "$gw" "$(curl -sS -o /dev/null -m 30 -w '%{http_code}' "https://$CID.ipfs.$gw/")"
+done
+```
+
+**6. The contract behind it is live:**
+
+```bash
+cast call $JAR "tipCount()(uint256)"   -r https://mainnet.base.org
+cast call $JAR "jarBalance()(uint256)" -r https://mainnet.base.org
+```
+
+**7. Finally, a real browser.** Open `$GW/`, connect a wallet on Base, and send one small tip
+(0.01 USDC is enough). Confirm the approve and the tip both land, and that the new entry appears at
+the top of the feed. Nothing else proves the RPC baked into the bundle actually works from a
+third-party network.
+
+---
+
+## Updating the site
+
+IPFS content is immutable. Any change — a copy tweak, a new RPC, a redeployed contract — means a new
+build, a new CID and a new upload. Repoint whatever name you publish under:
+
+```bash
+# DNSLink: set a TXT record on _dnslink.<your-domain>
+_dnslink.tips.example.com.  TXT  "dnslink=/ipfs/<new CID>"
+
+# ENS contenthash:
+# set the contenthash record on your ENS name to ipfs://<new CID>
+```
+
+Keep the previous CID pinned until the new one has propagated, then unpin it.
